  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\n\nThe rest of today through Tuesday will feature seasonably warm \ntemperatures across NE Montana accompanied by isolated to widely \nscattered afternoon and evening thunderstorms while ridging \nremains pronounced across the Northern High Plains. Chances for\nany specific location to receive any showers or storms through\ntomorrow is relatively low (<20%) but cannot be completely ruled\nout.\n\nToward the middle of the week a potentially significant pattern\nshift is expected to take place for northeast Montana. This \nshift will lead to increasing daily chances for showers and \nthunderstorms, with heavy rain potential as some southwestern\nmonsoonal moisture does transport its way this far north. There\nwill also be a major cooling with this pattern, where highs will\nbe in the 60s and 70s for many places as opposed to the current\n80s to 90s.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nDid not deviate from base forecast today. Confidence in any \nprecipitation amounts are still relatively low at this point, \nespecially given the significant jump in accumulation from \nprevious forecast iterations, but this could set up to be a low-\nconfidence/high- impact scenario where many locations could get \nnorth of 1\" of total rainfall this week. Beyond the rain, \nconfidence is moderate in the big temperature swing associated \nwith the major pattern shift that could bring the aforementioned \nrain. Once the system moves off to the east, confidence goes down \nwith respect to whether or not the region returns to warm and dry,\nor if chances for rain will persist into the weekend and next \nweek.\n\nCliff",
